---
// COOKBOOK — framework interop: SSR, hydration, islands (docs-refactor Phase 5, #1000; plan §8).
//
// The honest shape of this topic: the engine is a BROWSER runtime with a server-safe authoring
// surface. Nothing here invents an SSR API — the point is that the markup contract survives
// serialization and the runtime attaches afterwards, which is why islands work at all.
//
// This site is the worked example: an Astro static build, a persisted <field-root>, and a
// ClientRouter that swaps content without tearing the field down.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import SeeAlso from '../../../components/SeeAlso.astro';
import StatusChip from '../../../components/StatusChip.astro';
import CodeTabs from '../../../components/CodeTabs.astro';
import PlatformSupport from '../../../components/PlatformSupport.astro';

const ssrHtml = `<!-- Server-rendered, and complete without JavaScript. The contract is
     ATTRIBUTES, so it serializes: the body exists in the HTML the server sent. -->
<article data-body="attract" data-feedback data-hot>
  <h2>Quarterly report</h2>
</article>

<!-- The runtime attaches later and adopts what it finds. Nothing about the
     element changes on hydration — it was already a body, it just wasn't measured yet. -->
<field-root></field-root>`;

const reactJsx = `// React: the component owns the canvas and the overlay surface for you.
import { FieldField } from '@fundamental-engine/react';

export default function Layout({ children }) {
  return (
    <>
      <FieldField accent="#4da3ff" onReady={(f) => f.scan()} />
      {children}
    </>
  );
}`;

const islandJsx = `// An ISLAND that renders bodies must tell the field they arrived: the engine
// scans at boot, and an island hydrates after that.
useEffect(() => {
  field.scan();        // re-scan for the bodies this island just rendered
}, [items]);

// A body whose PARAMETERS change does not need a rescan — the reactive-param path
// applies within a frame:
handle.set({ strength: next });`;

const teardownJs = `// SPA navigation is where fields leak. A field owns a rAF loop, listeners and
// (contained) a ResizeObserver. Tear it down on unmount, every time.
useEffect(() => {
  const field = createField(canvas, { host: browserHost() });
  return () => field.destroy();
}, []);`;

const astroNote = `---
// This site: a static Astro build. The field is a persisted island — one <field-root>
// in the base layout, outside the swapped content, so client-side navigation changes
// the page WITHOUT restarting the engine.
---
<field-root render="none"></field-root>
<slot />`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Fundamental — Framework interop: SSR, hydration & islands"
  description="How the field behaves under server rendering, hydration and island architectures: the attribute contract serializes, the runtime attaches afterwards and adopts what it finds, islands re-scan after they hydrate, and every entry point must be torn down on unmount."
>
  <header class="docs-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Cookbook</p>
    <h1>Framework <span class="accent">interop</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The engine is a browser runtime with a <strong>server-safe authoring surface</strong>. That
      distinction is the whole topic: because a body is declared in attributes, it survives
      serialization and arrives in the HTML the server sent — and the runtime, whenever it attaches,
      simply adopts what it finds.
    </p>
    <StatusChip label="Shipped · this site is the worked example" tone="stable" />
  </header>

  <div class="callout">
    <b>There is no SSR API, and there should not be.</b> Nothing renders a field on the server: there
    is no canvas, no rAF and no viewport there. What survives the server is the
    <em>declaration</em> — and that is enough, because the field's contract is markup.
  </div>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="ssr">Server rendering</h2>
    <p>
      A server-rendered page is complete before any script runs: the content is there, the semantics
      are there, and the bodies are there as attributes. When the runtime attaches it scans and
      begins measuring. Nothing about the elements changes — they were already bodies; they just
      weren't being measured yet.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'HTML', lang: 'html', code: ssrHtml }]} />
    <p>
      This is also why <code>render: 'none'</code> is such a natural default for content sites: the
      no-JavaScript page and the hydrated page differ by a measurement, not by a layout.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="islands">Islands and hydration</h2>
    <p>
      An island hydrates <em>after</em> the field's initial scan, so bodies it renders are new to the
      engine. The rule is small and easy to get wrong in exactly one direction:
    </p>
    <ul class="model">
      <li>
        <b>New elements → <code>scan()</code>.</b> The field does not poll the DOM; tell it when the
        set of bodies changed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Changed parameters → nothing.</b> A body whose <code>strength</code> or <code>range</code>
        changes is picked up on the measure cadence. Use <code>handle.set(...)</code> for a
        programmatic body; re-scanning for this is waste.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>One field per page.</b> <code>&lt;field-root&gt;</code> is a page singleton. Two islands
        that each mount a page-wide field give you two engines writing the same variables — use one
        field and let islands contribute bodies, or scope each to its own
        <a href="/docs/cookbook/contained-fields">contained field</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <CodeTabs
      variants={[
        { label: 'React island', lang: 'tsx', code: islandJsx },
        { label: 'React component', lang: 'tsx', code: reactJsx },
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      <PlatformSupport dimension="field-handle-methods" symbol="rescan" note="after the DOM changes" />
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="teardown">Teardown is not optional</h2>
    <p>
      In a single-page app this is the bug that actually bites. A field owns a
      <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> loop, window listeners and — when contained — a
      <code>ResizeObserver</code>. A component that mounts a field and never destroys it leaks an
      engine per navigation, and the symptom is a page that grows steadily heavier rather than one
      that breaks.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'React', lang: 'tsx', code: teardownJs }]} />
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="this-site">This site, as the worked example</h2>
    <p>
      These docs are a static Astro build with client-side routing. The field is a
      <strong>persisted island</strong>: a single <code>&lt;field-root&gt;</code> in the base layout,
      outside the swapped content, so navigating between pages changes the document without
      restarting the engine — the field stays continuous across the transition.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'Astro layout', lang: 'astro', code: astroNote }]} />
    <p>
      Page-level runtimes follow the same discipline: each boots on load and on every navigation, and
      tears down before the swap, so nothing stacks. Every live demo in this cookbook is wired that
      way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <SeeAlso
    links={[
      { href: '/docs/cookbook', label: 'All cookbook patterns' },
      { href: '/docs/guides/react', label: 'The React guide' },
      { href: '/docs/guides/web-component', label: 'The web-component guide' },
      { href: '/docs/troubleshooting', label: 'Troubleshooting (SSR & hydration)' },
      { href: '/docs/cookbook/contained-fields', label: 'Contained fields' },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
